<p>The Trump administration has opened new Section 301 investigations into what it calls “unfair trade practices” by 16 major U.S. trading partners, including the European Union, China, India, Japan and Singapore. The probes start a process that could eventually lead to new tariffs. The move comes after a U.S. Supreme Court decision in February struck down President Trump’s earlier tariff approach. The administration is now looking for other legal routes to reapply trade pressure. Several outlets say the administration is pitching the probes as a response to unfair foreign practices. Critics and analysts warn they could raise trade tensions and, if tariffs follow, increase costs for businesses and consumers.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6upbshO6UqBaI4EJeS6WlhcGh8YVd7EF0hb8ty8DOhndXqgfByTPYlI-U64DCqweOrh7bmieFjcFERRXe_3L7ywhqec6qLTWY-NKpYqwBzh-cv2F59q6ZHfSklHj5QD5F7uHXTUaZs_vJDMSruxdT_scb2cK7eVqlW3NRGwqgx4HSMx_JIN1cG31h71eACwVqVXLrnl_ehKYZNksXCfSK3NCu_Mg9_5sF4e873k-3Rgd2HRzDANJplifXtdUNRg_0s0LSRBz3I3F2Qc4gw' alt='A worker lifts an aluminum beam on the factory floor at an Indiana manufacturer, as US trade policy shifts could affect industrial supply chains.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What the probes cover: The new Section 301 cases are described as manufacturing-focused investigations into foreign practices. The U.S. Trade Representative has launched them as the first step toward possible trade remedies.</li><li>Who’s under review: Reports name the EU and several major Asian economies — including China, India, Japan and Singapore — among the partners targeted in the new inquiries.</li><li>India exposure: Indian coverage says the probe could set the stage for new U.S. tariffs on India, depending on how the investigation unfolds.</li><li>Revenue rationale: Several outlets report the administration has pointed to replacing “hundreds of billions of dollars” in revenue it says was lost after the court ruling, as it searches for new tariff authority.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: The White House says it is launching manufacturing-focused investigations into foreign practices, framing Section 301 as a way to rebuild U.S. tariff leverage. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/11/trump-tariffs-investigation-supreme-court'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Trade policy coverage (Canada): Reporting highlights the legal workaround — using a different trade law to try to reestablish tariffs after the Supreme Court’s February ruling. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-trump-foreign-trade-tariffs-supreme-court/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Indian analysts/media: Indian explainers describe the investigation as a step that could open the door to tariffs on India and other partners, with spillover effects if penalties follow. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/us-trump-section-301-probe-india-impact-tariffs-13988635.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/trump-tariff-news-live-india-china-among-16-us-trading-partners-hit-by-trumps-301-tariff-probes-11203125#publisher=newsstand'>India, China Among 16 US Trading Partners Hit By Trump&#x27;s &#x27;301&#x27; Tariff Probes</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-trump-foreign-trade-tariffs-supreme-court/'>Trump administration opens trade investigation in bid to replace tariffs</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-administration-kicks-off-new-process-to-try-to-replace-tariffs-struck-down-by-supreme-court/article70733566.ece'>U.S. investigates India and China for ‘unfair trade practices’ as Trump tries to replace tariffs blocked by Supreme Court</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3346279/us-launches-trade-probe-china-and-eu-bid-revive-trump-tariffs'>US launches trade probe into China and EU in bid t